 lipase (LPL), an enzyme that breaks down triglycerides in chylomicrons and very low-density lipoproteins (VLDL)..", "Hepatology": "The scenario described involves a 13-month-old boy with hepatosplenomegaly and milky blood samples, which upon refrigeration show a creamy supernatant layer. The genetic analysis reveals a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2). This mutation is associated with a condition known as chylomicron retention disease (CRD), also referred to as chylomicronemia syndrome. ### Diagnosis and Explanation:\nChylomicron retention disease is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of chylomicrons (lipoproteins that transport dietary fats) in the blood. The milky appearance of the blood sample is due to the presence of chylomicrons, which are not properly cleared from the blood. The genetic mutation in APOC2 leads to a deficiency or dysfunction of apolipoprotein C-II, an enzyme that is crucial for the breakdown of chylomicrons. This results in the accumulation of chylomicrons in the blood, leading to hepatosplenomegaly and other complications. ### Complications:\n1. **Hepatomegaly and Splenomegaly**: The accumulation of chylomicrons can lead to fatty liver disease, which can progress to cirrhosis. The spleen may also be enlarged due to the accumulation of chylomicrons. 2..", "Genetics": "The medical scenario described involves a 13-month-old boy with hepatosplenomegaly (enlargement of the liver and spleen) and milky blood samples, which upon refrigeration show a creamy supernatant layer. The genetic analysis reveals a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) on chromosome 19. This mutation is associated with a condition known as chylomicron retention disease (CRD), also referred to as chylomicronemia syndrome. ### Diagnosis and Key Points\n\n**Chylomicron Retention Disease (CRD):**\nChylomicron retention disease is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of chylomicrons (lipoproteins that transport 